skill: REST APIs

@ HTTP Methods | B
Which HTTP method is normally used to retrieve a resource without changing it?
= GET
- POST
- PUT
- DELETE
? GET is safe and read-only by convention.

@ HTTP Methods | B
Which HTTP method is normally used to create a new resource?
= POST
- GET
- HEAD
- OPTIONS
? POST submits data to the server, typically creating a new resource under a collection.

@ HTTP Methods | A
Which HTTP methods are idempotent? (Select all that apply)
= GET
= PUT
= DELETE
- POST
? Repeating an idempotent request has the same effect as sending it once. POST usually creates a new resource each time.

@ HTTP Methods | I
What is the difference between PUT and PATCH?
= PUT replaces the whole resource while PATCH applies a partial update
- PUT applies a partial update while PATCH replaces the whole resource
- They are identical
- PATCH is used only to create resources
? Use PUT to send a complete representation and PATCH to change only some fields.

@ Status Codes | B
Which status code means a resource was created successfully?
= 201
- 200
- 204
- 301
? 201 Created is returned after a successful POST that creates a resource.

@ Status Codes | B
What does HTTP status 404 mean?
= The requested resource was not found
- The server crashed
- The request was not authorized
- The request succeeded with no content
? 404 Not Found means the URL does not map to an existing resource.

@ Status Codes | I
Which status code best represents missing or invalid authentication credentials?
= 401
- 403
- 404
- 500
? 401 Unauthorized means the caller is not authenticated.

@ Status Codes | I
Which status code means the user is authenticated but not allowed to perform the action?
= 403
- 401
- 400
- 502
? 403 Forbidden is returned when the identity is known but lacks permission.

@ REST Principles | I
Which REST constraint says each request must carry all the information needed to process it, with no server-side session state?
= Statelessness
- Cacheability
- Layered system
- Code on demand
? In a stateless API the server stores no client session between requests, which makes scaling easier.

@ REST Principles | I
Which URL follows REST naming conventions for fetching the orders of user 5?
= GET /users/5/orders
- GET /getOrdersByUser?id=5
- POST /fetchOrders/5
- GET /user_orders_list/5
? REST uses nouns for resources and HTTP methods for actions, not verbs in the URL.

@ JSON and Data Formats | B
Which header tells the server that the request body contains JSON?
= Content-Type: application/json
- Accept: text/html
- Cache-Control: no-store
- Authorization: Bearer
? Content-Type describes the format of the body that is being sent.

@ API Security | I
How is a JWT usually sent to call a protected API?
= In the Authorization header as a Bearer token
- Inside the URL path
- As a CSS class
- In the HTTP status line
? The standard form is Authorization: Bearer followed by the token.

@ API Security | A
What is the purpose of CORS?
= It controls which web origins a browser allows to call a server API
- It encrypts the request body
- It caches API responses
- It authenticates the user
? Browsers block cross-origin responses unless the server allows the origin using CORS headers.

@ Frontend-Backend Integration | I
A React app on localhost:5173 calls an API on localhost:8080 and the browser blocks the response. What is the most likely cause?
= The server does not allow the frontend origin in its CORS configuration
- The CSS file is missing
- The React version is too new
- The JSON is minified
? Different ports count as different origins, so the backend must list the frontend origin in its CORS settings.

@ Frontend-Backend Integration | I
Which is a good practice for an endpoint that returns a large list?
= Accept page and size parameters and return paging metadata
- Return every row in one response always
- Use POST to read the list
- Ignore any size sent by the client
? Pagination keeps responses small and predictable for both server and client.
